.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt EVP_PKEY_IS_A 3ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm EVP_PKEY_is_a ,
.Nm EVP_PKEY_can_sign ,
.Nm EVP_PKEY_type_names_do_all ,
.Nm EVP_PKEY_get0_type_name ,
.Nm EVP_PKEY_get0_description ,
.Nm EVP_PKEY_get0_provider
.Nd key type and capabilities functions
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/evp.h
.Ft int
.Fo EVP_PKEY_is_a
.Fa "const EVP_PKEY *pkey"
.Fa "const char *name"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo EVP_PKEY_can_sign
.Fa "const EVP_PKEY *pkey"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo EVP_PKEY_type_names_do_all
.Fa "const EVP_PKEY *pkey"
.Fa "void (*fn)(const char *name"
.Fa "void *data)"
.Fa "void *data"
.Fc
.Ft const char *
.Fo EVP_PKEY_get0_type_name
.Fa "const EVP_PKEY *key"
.Fc
.Ft const char *
.Fo EVP_PKEY_get0_description
.Fa "const EVP_PKEY *key"
.Fc
.Ft const OSSL_PROVIDER *
.Fo EVP_PKEY_get0_provider
.Fa "const EVP_PKEY *key"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn EVP_PKEY_is_a
checks if the key type of
.Fa pkey
is
.Fa name .
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_can_sign
checks if the functionality for the key type of
.Fa pkey
supports signing.
No other check is done, such as whether
.Fa pkey
contains a private key.
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_type_names_do_all
traverses all names for
.Fa pkey Ns 's
key type, and calls
.Em fn
with each name and
.Fa data .
For example, an RSA
.Sy EVP_PKEY
may be named both
.Qo Li RSA Qc
and
.Qo Li rsaEncryption Qc .
The order of the names depends on the provider implementation that holds
the key.
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get0_type_name
returns the first key type name that is found for the given
.Fa pkey .
Note that the
.Fa pkey
may have multiple synonyms associated with it.
In this case it depends on the provider implementation that holds the
key which one will be returned.
Ownership of the returned string is retained by the
.Fa pkey
object and should not be freed by the caller.
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get0_description
returns a description of the type of
.Sy EVP_PKEY ,
meant for display and human consumption.
The description is at the discretion of the key type implementation.
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get0_provider
returns the provider of the
.Sy EVP_PKEY Ns 's
.Xr EVP_KEYMGMT 3 .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn EVP_PKEY_is_a
returns 1 if
.Fa pkey
has the key type
.Fa name ,
otherwise 0.
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_can_sign
returns 1 if the
.Fa pkey
key type functionality supports signing, otherwise 0.
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get0_type_name
returns the name that is found or NULL on error.
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get0_description
returns the description if found or NULL if not.
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_get0_provider
returns the provider if found or NULL if not.
.Pp
.Fn EVP_PKEY_type_names_do_all
returns 1 if the callback was called for all names.
A return value of 0 means that the callback was not called for any
names.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Ss EVP_PKEY_is_a()
The loaded providers and what key types they support will ultimately
determine what
.Fa name
is possible to use with
.Fn EVP_PKEY_is_a .
We do know that the default provider supports RSA, DH, DSA and EC keys,
so we can use this as an crude example:
.Bd -literal
 #include <openssl/evp.h>

 ...
     /* |pkey| is an EVP_PKEY* */
     if (EVP_PKEY_is_a(pkey, "RSA")) {
         BIGNUM *modulus = NULL;
         if (EVP_PKEY_get_bn_param(pkey, "n", &modulus))
             /* do whatever with the modulus */
         BN_free(modulus);
     }
.Ed
.Ss EVP_PKEY_can_sign()
.Bd -literal
 #include <openssl/evp.h>

 ...
     /* |pkey| is an EVP_PKEY* */
     if (!EVP_PKEY_can_sign(pkey)) {
         fprintf(stderr, "Not a signing key!");
         exit(1);
     }
     /* Sign something... */
.Ed
